<%# The sharing model, which is the feature most likely to be misunderstood
    as "an app for families" when the actual claim is stranger and better:
    private *from* your family by default, with sharing as a visible act.
    D9's no-hidden-defaults rule is the whole page. %>

<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    One installation serves a household. Everyone in it gets their own
    account, and — this is the part worth reading twice — everything each
    person keeps is invisible to everyone else until they say otherwise.
  </p>
  <p>
    Not invisible to the internet. Invisible to your spouse, your adult
    children, your housemate, and the admin who set the thing up. Shared
    hardware is not shared visibility, and a household assistant that got
    that wrong would be worse than useless — it would be a surveillance
    device with a nice calendar.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">How sharing actually works</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "One thing at a time") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Share a single item with a single person: this appointment, this
        document, this task. They see it; nothing else comes with it; you
        can take it back later.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "A whole area, standing") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Or share an entire area — "everything I file under Money goes to my
        wife" — including things you file in future. Standing arrangements
        are how a couple actually runs a household, so they are a first-class
        option rather than a workaround.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Always visible, always revocable") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Settings lists every share you have granted, in one place, with a
        revoke button beside each. You should never have to wonder what
        you've shared, and you should never need to remember what you did
        last year.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Never guessed for you") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The app has no opinion about what a household ought to share. It
        does not decide that spouses see each other's money, or that parents
        see everything. There are no hidden defaults, because a default that
        shares something is a decision made on your behalf about your family.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What being shared with gets you</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Shared things behave like your own: they turn up on your calendar,
    appear in your searches, and count when the app looks across your life
    to tell you what needs attention. Two people who share their calendars
    get an assistant that can genuinely see the collision between the
    school run and the hospital appointment — which is the whole reason to
    put a household on one machine rather than five.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Anything the app does across people works strictly over what has been
    shared with the person asking. There is no view from above, and the
    admin account does not have one either.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Two things that stay sealed regardless</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    The password vault and the diary are encrypted with a key derived from
    your own login password. They are not merely un-shared — they are
    unreadable to anyone holding the database file, which includes the
    person who administers the machine and anyone who walks off with a
    backup. Sharing an individual password with the household is possible
    and deliberate; sharing the diary is not possible at all.
    <%= link_to "How that's built →", about_page_path("privacy"), class: "text-primary-700 hover:underline" %>
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Joining, and leaving</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    People join by an invitation link that the admin copies and hands over —
    no email server, nothing sent anywhere. People leave by having their
    account removed, and what they kept goes with them rather than being
    quietly inherited by the household.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    If a household stops being one household, the honest answer is that the
    data is a file: it can be copied, split, and taken. That is a great deal
    more than a subscription would give either of you.
  </p>
</section>
